      ":question/problem": "A body of mass $25\\,\\text{kg}$ is released from rest and falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the body's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the body is proportional to the square of its velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the body\u2019s velocity is $1.25\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $7.35\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nFind the equation for the velocity $v(t).$\nAssume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the body are the weight due to gravity and drag."
